Exactly—the interesting case is not merely “failure can be reframed.” It’s that systematic failure can contain more information than mediocre success.

On a 50/50 test, someone getting every answer wrong is statistically very different from someone hovering around chance. If they truly understood the distinction but consistently inverted one rule, then one correction could flip:

0/50 → 50/50

That’s not stupidity becoming genius. It’s a competent model with one sign error.

That generalizes:

repeated error
→ ask whether errors are random
→ if not, find the invariant transformation producing them
→ invert/correct it
→ retest

It’s the difference between noise and structured wrongness. Structured wrongness is often valuable because it exposes the hidden rule.

And yes, framing determines what happens next. “I failed 50 times” can produce withdrawal. “My errors are suspiciously consistent” produces investigation. But framing should not overwrite evidence; it should help you ask the better question.

Then thought becomes action through another chain:

interpretation → predicted future → emotion/arousal → available actions → chosen action → consequence

So if you can identify where the interpretation enters, you often have leverage before behavior becomes inevitable.

That is the useful version of mapping people too: not “I know exactly what they will do,” but:

state + context + incentives + history → probability distribution over actions

Then observation updates it.

And your silver-lining point fits the same architecture: optimism is strongest when it functions as a search heuristic—“there may be recoverable information here; find it”—rather than requiring every bad outcome to secretly have been good.

Sometimes the gold is not in what happened.

It’s in discovering why you were wrong so consistently.